EXT. EIFFEL TOWER - SECOND PLATFORM SCAFFOLD - DAY (1888)

Wind. Paris spreads below like a map. Iron beams ring with hammers.

She crouches on a plank, a bolt of grey wool across her knees, stitching a harness. ODETTE LACROIX, 22, seamstress, calloused fingertips, a scar on her chin.

Twenty feet off, he walks a girder like a tightrope, a rivet gun on his hip. JULES PERRIN, 25, rivetter, cap low, easy balance.

She doesn't look up. He grins anyway.

JULES
That one for me?

ODETTE
For whoever falls first.

He crosses the beam. Drops beside her. Close.

JULES
Then it's for you.

She bites the thread. Hands him the harness.

ODETTE
Put it on.

He does. She tightens the buckle with a brisk tug. He winces and smiles. She doesn't smile. Her eyes slide to the beam behind him.

A man's head rises from the ladder shaft. Bowler hat. Black coat. Soft hands. INSPECTOR MAXIME BOUCHARD, 50, with a notebook, climbing like a man who hates heights.

BOUCHARD
Monsieur Perrin?

Jules turns. Goes still. Odette's needle stops in the wool.

BOUCHARD (CONT'D)
The foreman says you were here Tuesday night.

JULES
I was home.

BOUCHARD
The foreman says otherwise.

Silence. The wind lifts his notebook pages. He grips them.

ODETTE
He was with me.

Bouchard looks at her. A long moment. Then he closes the notebook.

BOUCHARD
Of course he was.

He turns and descends the ladder. They watch the bowler vanish. She exhales. He lets his shoulders drop.

JULES
You didn't have to.

ODETTE
Yes.

She picks the needle back up.
